Below is a quick‑start guide you can drop into any page that contains a form.  
It uses the **typing‑animation SVG helpers** you have available (`createTypingSVG`, `setTypingAnimation`, `setCursorBlinkRate`, etc.) and works with plain‑HTML, React, Vue, or any other front‑end framework.

---

## 1. What the animation does

* When a user focuses a form field, a short “typing” SVG appears **above** the field and “writes” a helpful hint (e.g., “Enter your email address…”).  
* The SVG is a lightweight, self‑contained `<svg>` element, so it adds virtually no extra CSS/JS load.  
* The animation can be customized per‑field (font, colour, speed, delay, cursor blink).

---

## 2. Core workflow (pseudo‑code)

```text
1️⃣  Create the SVG once (or lazily on first focus) with createTypingSVG().
2️⃣  Position the SVG next to the input (absolute positioning inside a relative wrapper).
3️⃣  When the input receives focus:
      • Call setTypingAnimation() to start the typing (or just let the SVG start automatically).
      • Optionally adjust speed / delay for that field.
4️⃣  When the input blurs, hide or remove the SVG (or let it stay if you prefer).
```

---

## 3. Minimal HTML + vanilla‑JS example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Form with typing hint</title>
  <style>
    .field-wrapper {
      position: relative;               /* needed for absolute positioning of the SVG */
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .typing-hint {
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      top: -1.8rem;                     /* place it just above the input */
      pointer‑events: none;             /* let clicks go through to the input */
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<form id="demoForm">
  <div class="field-wrapper">
    <input type="email" id="email" placeholder="Email" autocomplete="off">
    <!-- placeholder for the SVG -->
    <div id="emailHint" class="typing-hint"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="field-wrapper">
    <input type="password" id="pwd" placeholder="Password" autocomplete="off">
    <div id="pwdHint" class="typing-hint"></div>
  </div>

  <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>

<script type="module">
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Helper to create a typing‑SVG and inject it
  // -------------------------------------------------
  async function makeHint({targetDiv, text, options = {}}) {
    const {fontColor, fontFamily, fontSize, typingSpeed} = options;
    const svg = await createTypingSVG({
      text,
      fontColor,
      fontFamily,
      fontSize,
      typingSpeed,
    });
    // The function returns an <svg> element (or a string – adjust as needed)
    targetDiv.innerHTML = '';          // clear any previous hint
    targetDiv.appendChild(svg);
    // Store the generated id so we can later tweak it
    const svgId = svg.id;
    // Optional: set cursor blink rate (default 500 ms)
    await setCursorBlinkRate({svgId, blinkRate: '500'});
    // Optional: change speed/delay after creation
    await setTypingAnimation({typingSpeed: typingSpeed ?? 60, delayBeforeStart: 300});
    return svgId;
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Attach focus/blur listeners to each field
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const fields = [
    {
      input: document.getElementById('email'),
      hintDiv: document.getElementById('emailHint'),
      hintText: 'Enter the email you use for your account.',
      options: {fontColor: '#0066cc', fontSize: 14, typingSpeed: 80},
    },
    {
      input: document.getElementById('pwd'),
      hintDiv: document.getElementById('pwdHint'),
      hintText: 'Choose a strong password (8+ characters).',
      options: {fontColor: '#0066cc', fontSize: 14, typingSpeed: 70},
    },
  ];

  fields.forEach(async ({input, hintDiv, hintText, options}) => {
    let svgId = null;

    input.addEventListener('focus', async () => {
      // Create the SVG only once per field
      if (!svgId) {
        svgId = await makeHint({targetDiv: hintDiv, text: hintText, options});
      }
      // Show the hint (in case it was hidden on blur)
      hintDiv.style.display = 'block';
    });

    input.addEventListener('blur', () => {
      // Hide the hint – you could also keep it visible if you prefer
      hintDiv.style.display = 'none';
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the script does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **`createTypingSVG`** | Generates an `<svg>` that animates the supplied `text`. All visual parameters (font, colour, size) are passed in. |
| **`setTypingAnimation`** | Adjusts the speed (words‑per‑minute) and optional start‑delay. In the example we use a 300 ms delay so the hint doesn’t pop up instantly. |
| **`setCursorBlinkRate`** | Sets how fast the cursor blinks (default 500 ms). You can make it faster for a “typing” feel. |
| **`focus` listener** | When the user clicks or tabs into the field, the hint SVG is created (once) and shown. |
| **`blur` listener** | Hides the hint when the field loses focus – you can change this to keep it visible if you like. |

---

## 4. Adapting to a framework

### React (functional component)

```jsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import {
  createTypingSVG,
  setTypingAnimation,
  setCursorBlinkRate,
} from './typingSvgHelpers';   // <-- wherever you expose the functions

export function TypingHintInput({ id, placeholder, hint, options }) {
  const wrapperRef = useRef(null);
  const svgIdRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const wrapper = wrapperRef.current;
    const hintDiv = wrapper.querySelector('.typing-hint');

    async function initHint() {
      const svg = await createTypingSVG({
        text: hint,
        ...options,
      });
      hintDiv.innerHTML = '';
      hintDiv.appendChild(svg);
      svgIdRef.current = svg.id;
      await setCursorBlinkRate({ svgId: svg.id, blinkRate: '500' });
      await setTypingAnimation({ typingSpeed: options?.typingSpeed ?? 60, delayBeforeStart: 300 });
    }

    initHint();
  }, [hint, options]);

  const handleFocus = () => {
    wrapperRef.current.querySelector('.typing-hint').style.display = 'block';
  };
  const handleBlur = () => {
    wrapperRef.current.querySelector('.typing-hint').style.display = 'none';
  };

  return (
    <div className="field-wrapper" ref={wrapperRef} style={{ position: 'relative' }}>
      <input
        id={id}
        placeholder={placeholder}
        onFocus={handleFocus}
        onBlur={handleBlur}
        autoComplete="off"
      />
      <div className="typing-hint" style={{ position: 'absolute', top: '-1.8rem', left: 0 }} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

*The component can be reused for any field – just pass a different `hint` string and optional styling options.*

### Vue 3 (Composition API)

```vue
